EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       The program below provides a simple example of the use of the functions
       described in this page.  The program creates a thread that executes a
       loop bracketed by pptthhrreeaadd__cclleeaannuupp__ppuusshh() and pptthhrreeaadd__cclleeaannuupp__ppoopp().
       This loop increments a global variable, _c_n_t, once each second.
       Depending on what command‐line arguments are supplied, the main thread
       sends the other thread a cancelation request, or sets a global variable
       that causes the other thread to exit its loop and terminate normally
       (by doing a _r_e_t_u_r_n).

       In the following shell session, the main thread sends a cancelation
       request to the other thread:

           $ ..//aa..oouutt;
           New thread started
           cnt = 0
           cnt = 1
           Canceling thread
           Called clean-up handler
           Thread was canceled; cnt = 0

       From the above, we see that the thread was canceled, and that the
       cancelation clean‐up handler was called and it reset the value of the
       global variable _c_n_t to 0.

       In the next run, the main program sets a global variable that causes
       other thread to terminate normally:

           $ ..//aa..oouutt xx;
           New thread started
           cnt = 0
           cnt = 1
           Thread terminated normally; cnt = 2

       From the above, we see that the clean‐up handler was not executed
       (because _c_l_e_a_n_u_p___p_o_p___a_r_g was 0), and therefore the value of _c_n_t was not
       reset.

       In the next run, the main program sets a global variable that causes
       the other thread to terminate normally, and supplies a nonzero value
       for _c_l_e_a_n_u_p___p_o_p___a_r_g:

           $ ..//aa..oouutt xx 11;
           New thread started
           cnt = 0
           cnt = 1
           Called clean-up handler
           Thread terminated normally; cnt = 0

       In the above, we see that although the thread was not canceled, the
       clean‐up handler was executed, because the argument given to
       pptthhrreeaadd__cclleeaannuupp__ppoopp() was nonzero.

